{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "name": "range-inverted",
  "description": "range_min > range_max on both channels (A: 200..0, B: 150..50). A transposed range is treated as a data-entry mistake, not a request to invert: both device.rs::scale_intensity and ParameterLinkConfig::ordered_range sort the endpoints, so 200..0 behaves exactly like 0..200 and 150..50 like 50..150. Output rises with input and a resting axis sits at the BOTTOM of the range. This fixture previously captured the opposite: scale_intensity returned `min` verbatim whenever max <= min, so channel A emitted 200 — device maximum — from the first tick with the axis at rest, and did so for every input. Use `curve: inverse` to invert; transposing the endpoints does not.",
